We have collateral pool with par balance 76,600,000. There is senior tranche A of 60,600,000 paying 4% interest per period on its outstanding balance. Junior tranche B of 4,900,000 paying 8% interest per period (B's principal not repaid during two periods). Equity receives whatever left after senior and junior interest and any OC diversions.

At each period, pool collects interest of 9% of its par balance at the start of period plus scheduled principal of $1,000,000. At end of each period, par falls by scheduled principal and by that period's defaults (period 1: 6,500,000; period 2: 1,300,000), with no recoveries.

Collections are paid in this order:

(1) senior interest on tranche A's balance at the start of the period;
(2) the overcollateralisation (OC) test: if end-of-period par divided by tranche A's balance is below 125%, cash is diverted to repay tranche A principal, exactly the amount that brings the ratio back to 125% (or all remaining cash if that is less);
(3) junior interest on tranche B, where any shortfall is lost, not carried forward;
(4) everything left goes to equity.

Tranche A's balance falls only through OC diversions.

